note intake.om: the Open Humanity intake, its own seeded Omega program. It runs outside the forged
note sandbox, because bringing a project means asking the Radicle node to seed and fetch it, and
note forged has no network and cannot run rad.
note   open-intake <open dir/> <state file> <work dir/> <radicle storage dir/> <home dir> [once]
note open dir: <data>open/ (queue written by forged; list.tsv published here, whole or not at all)
note state:    one decimal number, the queue offset already handled
note work dir: private, outside the folder forged can write: the list this program acts on
note           (list.tsv; the copy in the open dir is only what forged shows, never read back),
note           the allow file and command output
note Queue lines (from a less trusted process, so every field is checked again):
note   B <tab> rid <tab> did <tab> note     bring the project
note   W <tab> rid <tab> did               withdraw it (only the key that brought it)
note A queue read to its end past 262,144 bytes is renamed to queue.old, finished and removed, so
note forged's 1 MiB cap on the queue is never permanent.
note Bring: refused when the key is not in <work dir>allow, when the project is already on the
note shelf, when its storage folder already exists on this node (unless the project is held, below)
note or the node already has a seeding policy for it (a repository this node holds or seeds for
note another reason is never touched),
note when the key has 8 open projects or the shelf has 200. A folder left by this program's own
note failed fetch of the same project is removed first. While the row says that fetch failed, a
note refusal that is not about a fetch (key not invited, quota, node not answering) leaves the row
note as it is, so it cannot turn that leftover into a refusal for good. Then rad seed --scope all --fetch (90 s);
note not found is refused and unseeded; over 51,200 KiB is refused, unseeded and removed. The
note project's name and description come from its Radicle identity document.
note Held: a public project this node already holds and seeds by its own policy (rad inspect
note --visibility says public, rad seed lists it) is not fetched, seeded or changed; it gets a row
note in state "held", so the owner of the node and the keys it invited can put the node's own
note unfinished projects on the shelf. A stored folder the node does not seed (such as the leftover
note of this program's own failed fetch) is refused. Withdrawing a held row only marks it; the
note repository and its seeding policy stay as they were.
note Only a delegate of a held project (a key that rad inspect <rid> --delegates lists) may list
note it; any other key is refused, so nobody can put the node's own projects on the shelf under
note their key. A held row costs no fetch and no disk, so it does not count toward a key's 8 open
note projects (that quota counts rows in state open, the projects this program fetched); it does
note count toward the 200 on the shelf.
note Withdraw: rad unseed, remove the storage folder, mark the row withdrawn. Only a row that is
note "open" (or "held", which is only marked) in the private list, brought by the same key, can be
note withdrawn, so this program never unseeds or removes a repository it did not bring, whatever
note the open dir is made to say.
note The page snapshot of a listed project is <open dir>p/<rid>.txt (see "the snapshot" below);
note it is written when the project is listed, again when its lister brings it again, and removed
note when it is withdrawn.
note list.tsv rows: rid, state, did, date, kilobytes, name, description, note.
